## Why This Is Asked
Intersects BFS path counting and graph pruning under constraints; tests meticulous edge-case handling.

## Key Concepts
- BFS for unweighted shortest paths
- Path counting with modulo arithmetic
- Handling blocked nodes and unreachable cases
- Complexity: O(N+M)

## Code Example
```javascript
function shortestPaths(n, edges, blocked, s, t) {
  const g = Array.from({ length: n }, () => []);
  for (const [v, u] of edges) if (!blocked[v] && !blocked[u]) g[v].push(u);
  const dist = Array(n).fill(Infinity);
  const ways = Array(n).fill(0);
  if (blocked[s] || blocked[t]) return { dist: -1, ways: 0 };
  dist[s] = 0; ways[s] = 1;
  const q = [s];
  while (q.length) {
    const v = q.shift();
    for (const u of g[v]) {
      if (dist[u] === Infinity) {
        dist[u] = dist[v] + 1; ways[u] = ways[v]; q.push(u);
      } else if (dist[u] === dist[v] + 1) {
        ways[u] = (ways[u] + ways[v]) % 1000000007;
      }
    }
  }
  return { dist: dist[t] === Infinity ? -1 : dist[t], ways: ways[t] };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to weighted graphs and use Dijkstra with path counting?
- How to adapt for multiple sources and answering many queries efficiently?

## Why This Is Asked
Beginner DP concepts with a new angle: counting unordered combinations (coin change ways) and contrasting memoization with tabulation.

## Key Concepts
- Dynamic programming (top-down memoization)
- Tabulation (bottom-up DP)
- Unordered combinations vs permutations
- Space optimization (1D DP)

## Code Example
```javascript
function countWaysTopDown(coins, target) {
  const n = coins.length;
  const memo = new Map();
  function dfs(i, rem) {
    if (rem === 0) return 1;
    if (rem < 0 || i === n) return 0;
    const key = i + '|' + rem;
    if (memo.has(key)) return memo.get(key);
    const res = dfs(i + 1, rem) + dfs(i, rem - coins[i]);
    memo.set(key, res);
    return res;
  }
  return dfs(0, target);
}

function countWaysBottomUp(coins, target) {
  const dp = new Array(target + 1).fill(0);
  dp[0] = 1;
  for (const c of coins) {
    for (let r = c; r <= target; r++) {
      dp[r] += dp[r - c];
    }
  }
  return dp[target];
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you count permutations (order matters) instead of combinations?
- How would you handle very large targets or mod results to avoid overflow in languages with fixed integer sizes?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests understanding of handling duplicate-heavy inputs efficiently and the ability to implement in-place sorting algorithms with optimized space usage.

## Key Concepts
- **3-way partitioning**: Separates elements into three groups: less than, equal to, and greater than the pivot
- **In-place quicksort**: Achieves O(1) auxiliary space (excluding recursion stack)
- **Pivot selection**: Random sampling avoids worst-case behavior on sorted inputs; median-of-three provides more robust estimates
- **Complexity analysis**: Best/average O(n log n); worst O(n²) when pivot consistently produces imbalanced partitions

## Code Example
```javascript
function threeWayQuicksort(a, lo = 0, hi = a.length - 1) {
  if (lo >= hi) return;
  const pivot = a[Math.floor(Math.random() * (hi - lo + 1)) + lo];
  let lt = lo, i = lo, gt = hi;
  while (i <= gt) {
    if (a[i] < pivot) {
      [a[lt], a[i]] = [a[i], a[lt]];
      lt++; i++;
    } else if (a[i] > pivot) {
      [a[i], a[gt]] = [a[gt], a[i]];
      gt--;
    } else {
      i++;
    }
  }
  threeWayQuicksort(a, lo, lt - 1);
  threeWayQuicksort(a, gt + 1, hi);
}
```

## Complexity Analysis
- **Best case**: O(n log n) — partitions remain balanced
- **Average case**: O(n log n) — expected with random pivot
- **Worst case**: O(n²) — occurs with sorted input and poor pivot (e.g., always picking first or last element)

## Pivot Selection Trade-offs
| Strategy | Pros | Cons |
|----------|------|------|
| Random | Simple, avoids adversarial input | Still possible to pick poor pivots |
| Median-of-three | More robust estimates | Slightly more computation |

The 3-way partition is particularly valuable for arrays with many duplicates, as it reduces unnecessary swaps and comparisons for elements equal to the pivot.

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to formulate DP with non-trivial constraints (non-adjacent picks), carefully handle feasibility, and compare memoization vs bottom-up tabulation and space optimization.

## Key Concepts
- DP state (i, t) representing position and how many selections left
- Transitions: skip i (i+1,t) and take i (a[i] + f(i+2,t-1))
- Base conditions
- Impossibility when m > ceil(n/2)
- Space optimization via rolling arrays

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxSumNonAdjacent(a, m) {
  const n = a.length;
  const NEG = -Infinity;
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n+3 }, () => Array(m+1).fill(NEG));
  for (let i=0; i<=n; i++) dp[i][0] = 0;
  for (let i=n-1; i>=0; i--) {
    for (let t=1; t<=m; t++) {
      const skip = dp[i+1][t];
      const take = a[i] + dp[i+2][t-1];
      dp[i][t] = Math.max(skip, take);
    }
  }
  return dp[0][m];
}
```

