## Why This Is Asked
This question tests the ability to extend Dijkstra's algorithm to count shortest paths while handling ties and modular arithmetic—a common pattern in competitive programming and graph algorithms.

## Key Concepts
- Dijkstra's algorithm with path counting augmentation
- Non-negative edge weights, including zero-weight edges
- Modular arithmetic for large path counts and overflow prevention
- Edge case handling: unreachable destinations, multiple optimal paths, zero-weight cycles

## Code Example
```javascript
function countShortestPaths(n, edges, s, t) {
  const MOD = 1e9 + 7;
  const dist = new Array(n).fill(Infinity);
  const ways = new Array(n).fill(0);

  dist[s] = 0;
  ways[s] = 1;

  const pq = new MinPriorityQueue({ priority: x => dist[x] });
  pq.enqueue(s);

  while (!pq.isEmpty()) {
    const u = pq.dequeue().element;

    for (const [v, w] of edges[u]) {
      if (dist[u] + w < dist[v]) {
        dist[v] = dist[u] + w;
        ways[v] = ways[u];
        pq.enqueue(v);
      } else if (dist[u] + w === dist[v]) {
        ways[v] = (ways[v] + ways[u]) % MOD;
      }
    }
  }

  return { distance: dist[t] === Infinity ? -1 : dist[t], paths: ways[t] };
}
```

## Time & Space Complexity
- **Time**: O((N + M) log N) due to priority queue operations
- **Space**: O(N) for distance and path count arrays

## Why This Is Asked
Tests data-structure augmentation in a Trie to support prefix queries and k-th item retrieval with weights, mirroring autocomplete and ranking systems.

## Key Concepts
- Trie augmentation with per-subtree weights
- Prefix sum queries in O(length of prefix)
- K-th word selection within a prefix using cumulative weights
- Handling duplicates and deletions robustly

## Code Example
```javascript
class TrieNode { constructor(){ this.next=new Map(); this.subWeight=0; this.endWeight=0; this.isWord=false; } }
class Trie {
  constructor(){ this.root=new TrieNode(); }
  insert(word,w){ let node=this.root; node.subWeight+=w; for(let ch of word){ if(!node.next.has(ch)) node.next.set(ch,new TrieNode()); node=node.next.get(ch); node.subWeight+=w; } node.isWord=true; node.endWeight+=w; }
  delete(word,w){ this.insert(word,-w); }
  sumPrefix(prefix){ let node=this.root; for(let ch of prefix){ if(!node.next.has(ch)) return 0; node=node.next.get(ch); } return node.subWeight; }
  kthWord(prefix,k){ let node=this.root; for(let ch of prefix){ if(!node.next.has(ch)) return null; node=node.next.get(ch); }
    function dfs(n, path){ if(n.isWord && n.endWeight>0){ if(--k===0) return path; }
      for(let c of [...n.next.keys()].sort()){ let child=n.next.get(c); if(child.subWeight>=k){ const res=dfs(child, path+c); if(res) return res; } else { k-=child.subWeight; } }
      return null; }
    return dfs(node, prefix);
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle Unicode alphabets or memory limits?
- How would you support tie-breaking for equal weights?
- How would you optimize for bulk updates and high deletion rates?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to combine hash tables with heaps for real-time top-K queries under sliding-window expirations. It evaluates understanding of lazy cleanup techniques, time-based eviction strategies, and maintaining data consistency under concurrent updates.

## Key Concepts
- Hash map for O(1) task lookups and metadata storage
- Max-heap for efficient top-K ordering
- Lazy deletion to avoid expensive per-update heap removals
- Time-based expiration using timestamp validation

## Code Example
```javascript
class Leaderboard {
  constructor(W) {
    this.W = W;
    this.map = new Map(); // task_id -> {score, timestamp}
    this.heap = new MaxHeap(); // (score, task_id)
  }

  update(task_id, delta, ts) {
    const current = this.map.get(task_id) || { score: 0, timestamp: 0 };
    const updated = { 
      score: current.score + delta, 
      timestamp: ts 
    };
    this.map.set(task_id, updated);
    this.heap.push([updated.score, task_id]);
  }

  getTopK(k, current_ts) {
    const result = [];
    const temp = [];

    while (result.length < k && !this.heap.isEmpty()) {
      const [score, task_id] = this.heap.pop();
      const task = this.map.get(task_id);

      if (task && task.score === score && current_ts - task.timestamp <= this.W) {
        result.push({ task_id, score });
      } else {
        temp.push([score, task_id]);
      }
    }

    // Restore invalid entries
    temp.forEach(entry => this.heap.push(entry));

    return result;
  }
}
```

## Complexity Analysis
- **Update**: O(1) hash map + O(log n) heap push = O(log n)
- **Top-K query**: O(k log n) worst case due to lazy deletion
- **Space**: O(n) for hash map + heap (potentially O(n + m) where m are stale entries)
- **Expiration**: Handled during queries via timestamp validation

## Why This Is Asked

